--TEST--
Every `Core\Config::set` refusal answers `false` and leaves the value in force untouched — `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`, `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: tools:config/reading-and-changing-it-from-a-program-core-config
// `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`: a set the changeability class or a ceiling refuses returns `false`
// and leaves the value in place. Both halves are asserted here, because the
// half a bare `false` does not prove is that nothing moved — a member that
// refused *and* wrote would print identically on the first three lines.
Core\Config::set("memory", "128M");

// Not a quantity in the unit `memory` takes. The value has to spell the same
// thing the boot path would have accepted out of the file.
echo "unit=", (Core\Config::set("memory", "soon") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";

// `System`: `nvs.toml` is the only place it can be set, and the class answers
// that before any ceiling is consulted.
echo "system=", (Core\Config::set("opcache.validate", "false") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";

// And a name no directive row governs at all: `Core\Config` is the registry's
// surface, not a per-request string map.
echo "unknown=", (Core\Config::set("no.such.directive", "1") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";

// What survived all three: the one accepted set, and nothing else.
echo "value=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
echo "all=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Config::all()), "\n";

// None of which stops `restore` from putting the file's own value back — here,
// on a host with no file, nothing.
Core\Config::restore("memory");
echo "restored=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
--EXPECT--
unit=refused
system=refused
unknown=refused
value=128M
all=1
restored=unset
